WEBVTT

00:00.380 --> 00:01.643
- [Instructor] What we're
going to do in this video

00:01.643 --> 00:06.220
is tackle some word
problems involving ratios.

00:06.220 --> 00:08.675
So here, we're told that Yoda Soda

00:08.675 --> 00:10.833
is the intergalactic party drink

00:10.833 --> 00:15.561
that will have all of your
friends saying, mm, good this is.

00:15.561 --> 00:17.185
You are throwing a party

00:17.185 --> 00:21.352
and you need five liters of
Yoda Soda for every 12 guests.

00:22.870 --> 00:25.236
If you have 36 guests,

00:25.236 --> 00:29.155
how many liters of Yoda Soda do you need?

00:29.155 --> 00:33.108
So pause this video and try
to figure it out on your own.

00:33.108 --> 00:36.525
Well, they tell us the
ratio of liters of soda

00:36.525 --> 00:38.398
to number of guests.

00:38.398 --> 00:41.935
So you need five, it's five liters

00:41.935 --> 00:44.602
for every 12 guests is the ratio

00:45.528 --> 00:47.935
but we wanna have 36 guests.

00:47.935 --> 00:52.505
So if the ratio is five liters
of soda for every 12 guests

00:52.505 --> 00:55.321
but we're in a situation
where we have 36 guests,

00:55.321 --> 00:57.928
so this is three times as many guests,

00:57.928 --> 01:02.249
we're gonna need three times
as many liters of soda.

01:02.249 --> 01:05.058
So three times five is 15.

01:05.058 --> 01:06.563
Five to 12.

01:06.563 --> 01:08.190
Five liters for 12 guests

01:08.190 --> 01:10.393
or for five liters for every 12 guests

01:10.393 --> 01:14.018
is the same thing as 15
liters for every 36 guests.

01:14.018 --> 01:15.035
So to answer that question,

01:15.035 --> 01:16.717
how many liters do you need,

01:16.717 --> 01:18.300
you need 15 liters.

01:19.160 --> 01:21.660
Let's do another one of these.

01:23.999 --> 01:25.693
Here, we just have a picture

01:25.693 --> 01:27.484
of a bunch of fish in a tank

01:27.484 --> 01:29.482
and it says there are eight big fish

01:29.482 --> 01:32.142
for every blank small fish

01:32.142 --> 01:33.607
and then it says there are four big fish

01:33.607 --> 01:35.808
for every blank small fish.

01:35.808 --> 01:36.990
So pause this video again

01:36.990 --> 01:39.683
and see if you can work through this.

01:39.683 --> 01:43.233
Alright, so let's just
count the big fish first.

01:43.233 --> 01:45.397
So there's one, two, three,

01:45.397 --> 01:46.376
wait, let me count this way.

01:46.376 --> 01:49.306
One, two, three, four,
five, six, seven, eight.

01:49.306 --> 01:52.282
So in this tank, there
actually are eight big fish

01:52.282 --> 01:53.998
and so let's see how many
small fish there are.

01:53.998 --> 01:56.569
There's one, two, three, four, five,

01:56.569 --> 01:59.819
six, seven, eight, nine, 10 small fish.

02:01.049 --> 02:03.164
So in the tank, for every eight big fish

02:03.164 --> 02:06.345
which you see in red,
there are 10 small fish

02:06.345 --> 02:08.955
but here it says, there are four big fish

02:08.955 --> 02:10.622
for every blank small fish.

02:10.622 --> 02:12.223
So what would that be?

02:12.223 --> 02:13.389
Well, one way to think about it

02:13.389 --> 02:15.722
is we have half as many fish

02:17.190 --> 02:19.931
or half as many big fish
so we divided it by two.

02:19.931 --> 02:22.536
So we're gonna have
half as many small fish.

02:22.536 --> 02:24.042
So we're gonna divide by two.

02:24.042 --> 02:28.357
So for every four big fish,
there are five small fish

02:28.357 --> 02:29.254
and one way to think about it,

02:29.254 --> 02:31.338
you could divide the fish evenly

02:31.338 --> 02:33.334
into two groups right over here.

02:33.334 --> 02:35.456
So let's see, if we can capture,

02:35.456 --> 02:37.956
so if you could have, this is,

02:39.453 --> 02:40.798
if I divide it like that,

02:40.798 --> 02:45.071
here I have one, two, three, four big fish

02:45.071 --> 02:48.170
and one, two, three, four, five small fish

02:48.170 --> 02:51.557
then in this group I have
one, two, three, four big fish

02:51.557 --> 02:54.706
and one, two, three,
four, five small fish.

02:54.706 --> 02:58.296
So every four big fish,
there are five small fish.

02:58.296 --> 03:00.459
These are equivalent ratios.

03:00.459 --> 03:01.876
Let's keep going.

03:04.539 --> 03:07.433
So here, we're told an ice cream shop

03:07.433 --> 03:11.143
uses the following ingredients
to make one sundae.

03:11.143 --> 03:13.101
So they use two scoops of ice cream,

03:13.101 --> 03:15.461
four spoonfuls of sprinkles,

03:15.461 --> 03:17.909
two tablespoons of whipped cream.

03:17.909 --> 03:20.111
How many sundaes did the shop make

03:20.111 --> 03:23.361
if they used 32 spoonfuls of sprinkles?

03:25.286 --> 03:28.793
So pause the video and
try to think about it.

03:28.793 --> 03:31.205
So there's a couple of
ways to think about it.

03:31.205 --> 03:34.059
Here, it says, let's see,
we're talking about sprinkles.

03:34.059 --> 03:35.608
So that's what's relevant here.

03:35.608 --> 03:38.752
Four spoonfuls for every one sundae.

03:38.752 --> 03:40.229
So we could say that there,

03:40.229 --> 03:45.076
so the ratio of spoonfuls
to sundaes is four to one.

03:45.076 --> 03:48.326
Four spoonfuls, spoonfuls of sprinkles,

03:50.325 --> 03:51.208
let me write it this way,

03:51.208 --> 03:54.335
let me write sprinkles, sprinkles.

03:54.335 --> 03:56.241
How many spoonfuls?

03:56.241 --> 03:59.074
How many spoonfuls for one sundae?

04:01.634 --> 04:05.908
But here we're talking about
using 32 spoonfuls of sprinkles

04:05.908 --> 04:08.146
so that is eight times as many.

04:08.146 --> 04:09.941
So you're going to be able to use,

04:09.941 --> 04:12.633
create eight times as many sundaes.

04:12.633 --> 04:15.435
So you're gonna have 32
spoonfuls of sprinkles

04:15.435 --> 04:17.656
for every eight sundaes.

04:17.656 --> 04:19.486
So how many sundaes did the shop make?

04:19.486 --> 04:21.530
Well, they made eight.

04:21.530 --> 04:23.697
Let's do one last example.

04:26.261 --> 04:29.239
At a dog park, there are 10 black dogs,

04:29.239 --> 04:33.406
five brown dogs, two white
dogs and 12 multi-color dogs.

04:35.563 --> 04:39.730
For every one brown dog,
there are two blank dogs.

04:41.105 --> 04:44.614
Pause the video and figure
out what goes in this blank.

04:44.614 --> 04:46.248
Alright, so let's see.

04:46.248 --> 04:50.286
There's five brown dogs
for every 10 black dogs,

04:50.286 --> 04:53.020
five brown dogs for every two white dogs

04:53.020 --> 04:57.672
and five brown dogs for
every 12 multi-color dogs

04:57.672 --> 04:59.747
but here you're saying
for every one brown dog,

04:59.747 --> 05:02.518
there are two blank dogs.

05:02.518 --> 05:05.210
So what type of dog is there a ratio?

05:05.210 --> 05:06.431
So for every brown dog,

05:06.431 --> 05:09.450
there's twice as many of that type of dog.

05:09.450 --> 05:13.784
Well, here, we see for
every five brown dogs,

05:13.784 --> 05:15.784
there are 10 black dogs.

05:16.970 --> 05:17.867
So one way to think about it,

05:17.867 --> 05:19.213
the number of black dogs

05:19.213 --> 05:22.470
is always gonna be twice
the number of brown dogs.

05:22.470 --> 05:24.023
So for every one brown dog,

05:24.023 --> 05:27.856
there would be two, two
black, two black dogs.

05:29.973 --> 05:30.951
One way to think about it,

05:30.951 --> 05:33.934
the ratio between brown
dogs and black dogs

05:33.934 --> 05:35.235
and it's kinda counterintuitive.

05:35.235 --> 05:36.212
I used the wrong colors here.

05:36.212 --> 05:37.801
I should have used brown and black.

05:37.801 --> 05:39.023
So let me do that.

05:39.023 --> 05:41.178
So the ratio of brown to black

05:41.178 --> 05:45.011
is five brown dogs for
every 10, 10 black dogs

05:47.201 --> 05:50.382
or if you divide both of
these numbers by five,

05:50.382 --> 05:53.049
you would get one, one brown dog

05:54.256 --> 05:58.335
for every two, for every two black dogs

05:58.335 --> 06:01.670
and that's exactly what
this statement is saying.
